.TH TIMEKEEPER-CONFIG 5 "June 2026" "timekeeper 1.0" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
timekeeper-config \- configuration file format for timekeeper
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/timekeeper/config
.PP
.B ~/.config/timekeeper/config
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B timekeeper
can use a configuration file to set a default custom epoch for Arvelie date
encoding and decoding.
.PP
The epoch is the Gregorian date that begins Arvelie year
.BR 0000 .
When an epoch is configured, date commands use that epoch unless overridden by
the command line or disabled with
.BR --no-epoch .
.SH FILE LOCATION
.B timekeeper
checks for config files in this order:
.IP 1. 4
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/timekeeper/config
.IP 2. 4
.B ~/.config/timekeeper/config
.PP
The first available config file is used, unless overridden by an environment
variable or command-line option.
.SH FORMAT
The config file uses a simple key-value format:
.PP
.RS
epoch=YYYY-MM-DD
.RE
.PP
Example:
.PP
.RS
epoch=1990-05-12
.RE
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B epoch
Sets the default custom epoch date. The value must be a valid Gregorian date in
the form
.IR YYYY-MM-DD .
.SH EXAMPLES
Create a config file:
.PP
.RS
mkdir -p ~/.config/timekeeper
.br
printf "epoch=1990-05-12\\n" > ~/.config/timekeeper/config
.RE
.PP
Then run:
.PP
.RS
timekeeper encode-date 1990-05-12
.br
0000A01
.RE
.PP
Disable the configured epoch for a single command:
.PP
.RS
timekeeper --no-epoch encode-date 1990-05-12
.br
1990J06
.RE
.SH OVERRIDES
Epoch source priority is:
.IP 1. 4
Command-line option:
.B --epoch
.I YYYY-MM-DD
or
.BI --epoch= YYYY-MM-DD
.IP 2. 4
Environment variable:
.B ARVELIE_EPOCH
.IP 3. 4
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/timekeeper/config
.IP 4. 4
.B ~/.config/timekeeper/config
.PP
Command-line values override environment variables and config files.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ARVELIE_EPOCH
Sets the default epoch without using a config file.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR timekeeper (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Written for the timekeeper project.
